## Summing Sequences

### Sum from 1 to n

The sum of 1 + 2 + 3 + ... + n is a classic loop pattern:

```xiom
fn sum_to(n: Int) -> Int {
  if n < 1 {
    return 0;
  }
  var total = 0;
  var i = 1;
  while i <= n {
    total = total + i;
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return total;
}
```

### Tracing sum_to(5)

| i | total | calculation |
|---|-------|-------------|
| 1 | 1     | 0 + 1 = 1  |
| 2 | 3     | 1 + 2 = 3  |
| 3 | 6     | 3 + 3 = 6  |
| 4 | 10    | 6 + 4 = 10 |
| 5 | 15    | 10 + 5 = 15|

Result: 15. And indeed 1+2+3+4+5 = 15.

### Sum Any Range

What if you want to sum from 10 to 20, not just from 1?

```xiom
fn sum_range(start: Int, end: Int) -> Int {
  if start > end {
    return 0;
  }
  var total = 0;
  var i = start;
  while i <= end {
    total = total + i;
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return total;
}
```

### Using Both Functions

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let s1 = sum_to(10);
  let s2 = sum_range(10, 20);
  let s3 = sum_to(100);

  io.println("All sums calculated!");

  return 0;
}
```

### The Power of Parameters

`sum_to(n)` is actually just `sum_range(1, n)` — the more general function can do everything the specific one can. But having both makes your intent clear: `sum_to(100)` reads better than `sum_range(1, 100)`.

